# AUTHORITATIVE IMPLEMENTATION SPEC — C++ `indicators` test5 → Rust

Everything below was built and verified end-to-end in a throwaway tree (`/tmp/flat`, never
`/output`) on rustc/cargo 1.75.0. Both build paths were run with warnings-as-errors and both
binaries `cmp`-clean against `/workspace/dataset/test5_executable` (29 bytes, exit 0, argv-invariant).
The code blocks below are the verified artifact, not a sketch — copy them verbatim.

---

## 0. TARGET CONTRACT (restated, re-verified)

```
30.0%\n 1/3\n 33.3%\n 33.0%\n 66.00%\n   = 6+4+6+6+7 = 29 bytes, exit 0
xxd: 3330 2e30 250a 312f 330a 3333 2e33 250a 3333 2e30 250a 3636 2e30 3025 0a
```
Re-verified in this session, including with `foo bar 42` appended.

---

## 1. FILE LAYOUT (FLAT — decision, see §7.1)

```
/output/Cargo.toml
/output/test5.rs                          <- package-root entry point (bin target)
/output/src/indicators/mod.rs             <- module hub + re-exports + ratio()
/output/src/indicators/format.rs          <- Format::percentage{,_with_precision}
/output/src/indicators/progress_builder.rs<- ProgressBuilder
/output/src/indicators/progress_tracker.rs<- ProgressTracker
```
No `src/main.rs`. No `src/lib.rs`. No `[lib]` target. No `.gitignore` needed (optional).
Do not create `src/indicators/progress/` — the flat split matches the deliverable's own example
and a 3-type library needs no extra namespace level.

---

## 2. /output/Cargo.toml  (verbatim, verified)

```toml
[package]
name = "test5"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

[[bin]]
name = "test5"
path = "test5.rs"
```

`cargo metadata --no-deps` must show exactly one target: kind `["bin"]`, name `test5`,
src_path `/output/test5.rs`. Omitting `[[bin]]` is a hard manifest error ("no targets specified");
adding a `[lib]` over `src/indicators/mod.rs` breaks `cargo build` only (E0432, because mod.rs
becomes a second crate root) while bare rustc still succeeds — do not do it.

---

## 3. /output/src/indicators/mod.rs  (verbatim, verified)

```rust
//! Rust port of the C++ `indicators` header, reconstructed black-box from
//! test5.cpp's call sites plus the reference binary's 29 bytes of stdout.

pub mod format;
pub mod progress_builder;
pub mod progress_tracker;

pub use self::format::Format;
pub use self::progress_builder::ProgressBuilder;
pub use self::progress_tracker::ProgressTracker;

/// Completion fraction shared by `ProgressBuilder::build` and
/// `ProgressTracker::get_percentage`.
///
/// Divide first in `f32`, then let `Format::percentage` multiply by 100 — this is
/// the order the reference binary uses, and it is float-only, so `total == 0`
/// yields inf/NaN instead of panicking (an integer `progress * 100 / total`
/// would panic in Rust).
pub(crate) fn ratio(progress: i32, total: i32) -> f32 {
    progress as f32 / total as f32
}
```

**Public items**
| Item | Signature | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `format`, `progress_builder`, `progress_tracker` | `pub mod` | submodule declarations, no `#[path]` needed (they resolve relative to `src/indicators/`) |
| re-exports | `pub use self::format::Format;` etc. | flatten to `indicators::{Format, ProgressBuilder, ProgressTracker}` so `test5.rs` reads like the C++ `indicators::X` call sites |
| `ratio` | `pub(crate) fn ratio(progress: i32, total: i32) -> f32` | returns `progress as f32 / total as f32`. No zero check (§8.1). |

**MANDATORY, and the one thing every analyst under-specified:** the `self::` prefixes are
required, not stylistic. Bare `rustc test5.rs` compiles in **edition 2015**, where a `use` path
in a non-root module is crate-relative. `pub use format::Format;` therefore fails with
`error[E0432]: unresolved import 'format'` under rustc while compiling fine under cargo/2021 —
i.e. it breaks build path (a) only. I hit this exact error and fixed it. Use `self::` for
re-exports and `super::` for sibling imports everywhere; both forms are valid in 2015 and 2021.

---

## 4. /output/src/indicators/format.rs  (verbatim, verified)

```rust
//! Port of the C++ `indicators::Format` static-member helper.

/// The C++ default argument on `percentage(float value, int precision = 1)`.
pub const DEFAULT_PRECISION: usize = 1;

/// Stateless holder for the formatting helpers, mirroring the C++ class that
/// only ever exposes static members.
pub struct Format;

impl Format {
    /// C++ `Format::percentage(value)` — the call form that takes the default
    /// `precision = 1`. Line 4 of the expected output ("33.0%") uses it.
    pub fn percentage(value: f32) -> String {
        Self::percentage_with_precision(value, DEFAULT_PRECISION)
    }

    /// C++ `Format::percentage(value, precision)`:
    /// `oss << std::fixed << std::setprecision(precision) << (value * 100.0f) << "%"`.
    ///
    /// The multiply happens in `f32` (the product is rounded back to `f32`) and is
    /// then widened to `f64` for rendering, mirroring the `mulss` + `cvtss2sd`
    /// pipeline of an `ostream << float` insertion. Rust's `{:.N$}` is the exact
    /// equivalent of `std::fixed` + `setprecision(N)`: exactly N fractional digits,
    /// never scientific notation, trailing zeros preserved, round-half-to-even on
    /// the exact binary value.
    pub fn percentage_with_precision(value: f32, precision: usize) -> String {
        let scaled = f64::from(value * 100.0f32);
        format!("{scaled:.precision$}%")
    }
}
```

**Public items**
| Item | Signature | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `DEFAULT_PRECISION` | `pub const DEFAULT_PRECISION: usize = 1;` | names the C++ default argument instead of burying a magic `1`. |
| `Format` | `pub struct Format;` | unit struct, zero fields, no `new()`, no `Default`. Namespace holder only. |
| `Format::percentage` | `pub fn percentage(value: f32) -> String` | `percentage_with_precision(value, DEFAULT_PRECISION)`. Nothing else. |
| `Format::percentage_with_precision` | `pub fn percentage_with_precision(value: f32, precision: usize) -> String` | `format!("{scaled:.precision$}%")` where `scaled = f64::from(value * 100.0f32)`. No clamping, no rounding, no inf/NaN special-casing, no trailing-zero trimming. |

**Default-argument decision (required by the brief):** two named functions, `percentage(v)`
delegating to `percentage_with_precision(v, precision)`. Do **not** use a `Precision` trait /
generic `impl Into<Option<usize>>` / builder-style overload trick. The two-function split is what
a Rust reviewer expects, mirrors the C++ ABI (the reference binary has one symbol,
`Format::percentage(float, int)`, with the constant `1` materialized by the caller — there is no
default-argument thunk), and keeps both arities visible at the call site so line 4 vs line 5 is
self-documenting. A trait trick would buy nothing and hide which precision line 4 uses.

**`precision: usize`, not `i32`.** Rust's `{:.N$}` consumes a `usize`; a negative precision is
unrepresentable in Rust formatting at all, so typing it `i32` would only invite an `as usize` cast
that can wrap. Only 1 and 2 are ever passed. (The C++ parameter is `int`; a negative
`setprecision` there falls through to snprintf's default 6 digits. Unreachable and out of scope.)

**Exact format string, mandated:** `"{scaled:.precision$}%"`. Equivalent and acceptable only if you
prefer them: `format!("{:.*}%", precision, scaled)` or `format!("{:.prec$}%", scaled, prec = precision)`.
I verified all three produce identical bytes for all five lines under bare rustc (edition 2015) —
inline captured named args work in every edition since 1.58. Pick the named form; `{:.*}` reads
like a puzzle.

---

## 5. /output/src/indicators/progress_builder.rs  (verbatim, verified)

```rust
use super::{ratio, Format};

/// Port of C++ `indicators::ProgressBuilder`.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
pub struct ProgressBuilder {
    progress: i32,
    total: i32,
}

impl ProgressBuilder {
    /// C++ `indicators::ProgressBuilder builder;`
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self { progress: 0, total: 100 }
    }

    /// Chainable, mirroring `builder.set_progress(60).set_total(200);`.
    pub fn set_progress(&mut self, value: i32) -> &mut Self {
        self.progress = value;
        self
    }

    /// Chainable, mirroring `builder.set_progress(60).set_total(200);`.
    pub fn set_total(&mut self, value: i32) -> &mut Self {
        self.total = value;
        self
    }

    /// Renders the completion percentage, e.g. `{60, 200}` -> `"30.0%"`.
    /// Delegates to `Format::percentage`, i.e. one decimal place.
    pub fn build(&self) -> String {
        Format::percentage(ratio(self.progress, self.total))
    }
}

impl Default for ProgressBuilder {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self::new()
    }
}
```

**Public items**
| Item | Signature | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `ProgressBuilder` | `#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)] pub struct ProgressBuilder { progress: i32, total: i32 }` | private fields, both `i32` (C++ `int`; keeps negative totals representable, unlike `usize`). |
| `new` | `pub fn new() -> Self` | `{ progress: 0, total: 100 }`. |
| `Default` | `impl Default for ProgressBuilder` | hand-written, forwards to `new()`. **Cannot** be derived: `total` defaults to 100, not 0. |
| `set_progress` | `pub fn set_progress(&mut self, value: i32) -> &mut Self` | stores, returns `self`. |
| `set_total` | `pub fn set_total(&mut self, value: i32) -> &mut Self` | stores, returns `self`. |
| `build` | `pub fn build(&self) -> String` | `Format::percentage(ratio(self.progress, self.total))`. Returns an owned `String` containing the trailing `%`; it is **not** a bar, not a stream manipulator, not a `Display` wrapper. |

`&mut self -> &mut Self` is mandated by the brief and by the call site
`builder.set_progress(60).set_total(200);`. A by-value `mut self -> Self` builder would compile but
force `let builder = ProgressBuilder::new().set_progress(60).set_total(200);`, changing the
statement shape of the ported `main`. Do not use it.

Defaults `{0, 100}` are the one genuinely unobservable choice here (both fields are overwritten
before any print). They match the tracker's defaults, which analyst 1 read directly out of
`.rodata` as the 8 bytes `00 00 00 00 64 00 00 00`. They cannot affect any output byte.

---

## 6. /output/src/indicators/progress_tracker.rs  (verbatim, verified)

```rust
use super::{ratio, Format};

/// Port of C++ `indicators::ProgressTracker`.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct ProgressTracker {
    progress: i32,
    total: i32,
    stages: Vec<String>,
}

impl ProgressTracker {
    /// C++ `indicators::ProgressTracker tracker;`
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self { progress: 0, total: 100, stages: Vec::new() }
    }

    /// Chainable, mirroring
    /// `tracker.add_stage("Downloading").add_stage("Processing").add_stage("Saving");`.
    pub fn add_stage(&mut self, name: &str) -> &mut Self {
        self.stages.push(name.to_string());
        self
    }

    /// Called as a standalone statement in test5.cpp, so it mirrors C++ `void`.
    pub fn set_progress(&mut self, value: i32) {
        self.progress = value;
    }

    /// Called as a standalone statement in test5.cpp, so it mirrors C++ `void`.
    pub fn set_total(&mut self, value: i32) {
        self.total = value;
    }

    /// The stages are recorded and never read back — the C++ program stores three
    /// of them and prints none. This accessor is the only reader of the field, and
    /// nothing in a binary crate can reach it, so rustc reports it as dead code;
    /// the allow is scoped to this one item rather than muting the whole crate.
    #[allow(dead_code)]
    pub fn stages(&self) -> &[String] {
        &self.stages
    }

    /// `"{progress}/{total}"`, e.g. `{1, 3}` -> `"1/3"`.
    pub fn get_progress(&self) -> String {
        format!("{}/{}", self.progress, self.total)
    }

    /// Renders the completion percentage, e.g. `{1, 3}` -> `"33.3%"`.
    pub fn get_percentage(&self) -> String {
        Format::percentage(ratio(self.progress, self.total))
    }
}

impl Default for ProgressTracker {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self::new()
    }
}
```

**Public items**
| Item | Signature | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `ProgressTracker` | `#[derive(Debug, Clone)] pub struct ProgressTracker { progress: i32, total: i32, stages: Vec<String> }` | field order mirrors the observed layout (`progress` @+0, `total` @+4, vector @+8). Not `Copy` (owns a `Vec`). |
| `new` | `pub fn new() -> Self` | `{ progress: 0, total: 100, stages: Vec::new() }` — defaults directly observed in `.rodata`. |
| `Default` | `impl Default for ProgressTracker` | hand-written, forwards to `new()`; **not** derivable (`total = 100`). |
| `add_stage` | `pub fn add_stage(&mut self, name: &str) -> &mut Self` | `self.stages.push(name.to_string())`, returns `self`. Chainable — three chained calls at the call site prove the fluent return. `&str` is the right parameter type (the C++ takes `const std::string&`; `&str` is its idiomatic Rust counterpart and accepts the string literals directly). Do not use `impl Into<String>` / generics — needless. |
| `set_progress` | `pub fn set_progress(&mut self, value: i32)` | stores. Returns `()`. |
| `set_total` | `pub fn set_total(&mut self, value: i32)` | stores. Returns `()`. |
| `stages` | `pub fn stages(&self) -> &[String]` (with `#[allow(dead_code)]`) | returns the recorded stage names. Never called by `main`. |
| `get_progress` | `pub fn get_progress(&self) -> String` | `format!("{}/{}", self.progress, self.total)`. Plain `i32` Display on both sides of a literal `/`: no width, no padding, no separators, no percent sign, no floats. |
| `get_percentage` | `pub fn get_percentage(&self) -> String` | `Format::percentage(ratio(self.progress, self.total))` — one decimal place plus `%`. |

**Forbidden inventions:** no `current_stage`, `advance`, `next_stage`, `render`, `bar`, `width`,
`fill_char`, `Display`/`Iterator` impls, no `stage_count`, no error types. Nothing in the call
sites or the 29 bytes implies them; adding them is fabricated API.

---

## 7. /output/test5.rs  (verbatim, verified — the entry point)

```rust
#[path = "src/indicators/mod.rs"]
mod indicators;

use indicators::{Format, ProgressBuilder, ProgressTracker};

fn main() {
    // The C++ `main(int argc, char* argv[])` never touches either parameter and its
    // output is unchanged by extra arguments, so collect and ignore them: no
    // indexing, no parsing, no validation, no early exit.
    let _args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();

    let mut builder = ProgressBuilder::new();
    builder.set_progress(60).set_total(200);
    println!("{}", builder.build());

    let mut tracker = ProgressTracker::new();
    tracker
        .add_stage("Downloading")
        .add_stage("Processing")
        .add_stage("Saving");
    tracker.set_progress(1);
    tracker.set_total(3);
    println!("{}", tracker.get_progress());
    println!("{}", tracker.get_percentage());

    println!("{}", Format::percentage(0.33f32));
    println!("{}", Format::percentage_with_precision(0.66f32, 2));
}
```

Notes that are load-bearing:
- The single `#[path = "src/indicators/mod.rs"]` is resolved relative to the **containing file's
  directory** (`/output`), which is why both build paths work; submodules below it need no
  `#[path]`. Corollary: the executable must be produced with `cd /output && rustc test5.rs`, since
  rustc writes the binary into the *cwd*.
- Five `println!` calls = exactly five `\n`, one trailing newline, no sixth. `std::endl`'s flush
  changes no bytes; stdout is flushed at exit, so no manual `flush()`.
- `main` returns `()` — falls off the end, exit code 0. No `std::process::exit`, no `-> Result`.
- Literals must be `0.33f32` / `0.66f32` (not `0.33` inferred as f64, not `0.33f64`) so the values
  are the `float`s the C++ compiler emitted.
- Bind as `_args`, not `args` — a plain `args` binding warns `unused variable`.
- `let mut builder` / `let mut tracker` are required by `&mut self`; the C++ objects are likewise
  mutated.
- Do **not** build the stage list from an array literal and call `.into_iter()` on it: that emits
  the `array_into_iter` warning under edition 2015 (bare rustc) while staying silent under
  cargo/2021. Three chained `add_stage` calls mirror the C++ anyway.
- Do **not** put `#![allow(dead_code)]` at the crate root. Verified unnecessary with the API above.

### 7.1 Resolved disagreements (every one, explicitly)

| # | Disagreement | Decision | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Module layout: analyst 3's nested `src/indicators/progress/{mod,builder,tracker}.rs` vs the brief's flat example | **Flat**: `format.rs`, `progress_builder.rs`, `progress_tracker.rs` | Matches the deliverable's own example; the extra level would exist only to host `ratio()`. Validated on both build paths. |
| 2 | Arithmetic: analyst 3's pure-`f32` `v * 100.0` vs analyst 1/2's `f32` multiply then `as f64` | **`f64::from(value * 100.0f32)`** | `f32` multiply keeps latent behavior aligned with the C++ `float` pipeline; the widen mirrors `ostream << float`'s `cvtss2sd`. Analyst 2 proved the widen is byte-neutral (31,457,280 exhaustive comparisons over the `[30,72]` f32 band, 0 differences; `f32`→`f64` is exact and both formatters expand exactly). `f64::from` over `as f64` because it is the lossless `From` impl, not a truncating cast. |
| 3 | `ProgressTracker::set_progress`/`set_total` return type: `()` vs `&mut Self` | **`()`** | Both call sites are standalone statements; nothing observable returns a reference. `()` mirrors C++ `void` and is the honest minimum. The asymmetry with `add_stage` (which *is* chained three deep) is evidence-driven, and the doc comments say so. Inventing a fluent return to look tidy would assert an unobserved API. |
| 4 | Dead code: analyst 3's crate-level `#![allow(dead_code)]` vs analyst 1's honest accessor | **Neither alone: minimal API + one item-scoped `#[allow(dead_code)]` on `stages()`** | Verified: with the API in §4–6 and no crate-level allow, `rustc -D warnings test5.rs` and `RUSTFLAGS="-D warnings" cargo build --release` both exit 0. A crate-wide allow would also hide *future* real dead code. Drop the speculative `progress()`/`total()`/`stage_count()` getters (they warn and nothing motivates them); keep `stages()` so the field has a reader, and scope the allow to it. |
| 5 | Exact value of `0.33f32`: analyst 1 says `0.32999999821186065673828125`, analyst 2 says `0.3300000131130218505859375` (and that the brief's premise is wrong) | **Analyst 2 is right** | Re-verified this session: `0.33f32` = bits `0x3ea8f5c3`, exact `0.3300000131130218505859375` — it rounds **up**. Analyst 1's decimal is `nextbelow(0.33f)`'s neighborhood and is not that bit pattern. Consequence is nil (`0.33f32 * 100.0f32 == 33.0f32` exactly, bits `0x42040000`), but the spec must not carry the wrong constant. |
| 6 | Default-precision expression: second function vs a generic/trait trick | **Second function** (`percentage` → `percentage_with_precision`) | Brief's stated preference, and the trick has no upside here. |
| 7 | Order of operations for derived percentages | **Divide first, then multiply by 100** | Both analysts' disassembly agrees (`cvtsi2ssl; cvtsi2ssl; divss; mulss`), and the folded constant `0x403E000020000000` = 30.0000019073486328125 in the reference binary only matches `(60/200)*100`, not `(60*100)/200`. Encoded once, in `ratio()`. |
| 8 | Whether `build()`/`get_percentage()` delegate to `Format::percentage` or inline its body | **Delegate** | Behaviorally identical, DRY, and the inlined machine code is an exact clone of `Format::percentage`'s body. |
| 9 | Field types: `i32` vs `usize`/`u64` | **`i32`** | C++ `int`, sign-extended in the disassembly. `usize` would render `total = -3` as `18446744073709551615` on line 2. |
| 10 | `precision` type: `int`/`i32` vs `usize` | **`usize`** | Required by `{:.N$}`; negative precision is unrepresentable in Rust formatting regardless. |

### 7.2 Per-line evaluation (all five, exact)

| Line | Bytes | Expression in `main` | Value pipeline |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `30.0%` | `builder.build()` | `60f32/200f32` = `0x3e99999a`; `*100.0f32` = `0x41f00001` = 30.0000019073486328125 (**not** exactly 30.0); `f64::from` exact; `{:.1}` → `30.0`; `+ '%'`. |
| 2 | `1/3` | `tracker.get_progress()` | `format!("{}/{}", 1i32, 3i32)`. No FP. |
| 3 | `33.3%` | `tracker.get_percentage()` | `1f32/3f32` = `0x3eaaaaab`; `*100.0f32` = `0x42055556` = 33.33333587646484375; `{:.1}` → `33.3`. |
| 4 | `33.0%` | `Format::percentage(0.33f32)` | `0.33f32` = `0x3ea8f5c3`; `*100.0f32` rounds to **exactly** `33.0f32` (`0x42040000`); `{:.1}` → `33.0`. Precision comes from `DEFAULT_PRECISION` = 1. |
| 5 | `66.00%` | `Format::percentage_with_precision(0.66f32, 2)` | `0.66f32` = `0x3f28f5c3`; `*100.0f32` rounds to **exactly** `66.0f32` (`0x42840000`); `{:.2}` → `66.00`. Proves precision is a real parameter and that trailing zeros are preserved — any shortest-repr or zero-trimming helper prints `66%` and fails. |

**Why the chosen arithmetic cannot alter these five lines** (analyst 2, three independent proofs):
(1) 18 direct C++-vs-Rust format cases covering every path per line diffed byte-identical;
(2) the same modular program built pure-`f32`, widen-then-`f64`, and all-`f64` each emitted the
identical 29 bytes under `cmp` against the reference; (3) margin analysis — the tightest distance
to a rounding-decision boundary among the five values is line 5's 0.005 absolute = **655 f32 ULPs**
(lines 1/3/4: 26213 / 4368 / 13107 ULPs), while the largest disagreement between candidate paths is
one ULP of the product (≤7.63e-06), roughly three orders of magnitude too small to move a digit.
Round-half-to-even agreement is structural, not luck: both glibc `%.*f` and Rust `{:.N$}` do exact
binary→decimal expansion then round at digit N (proof: `1e30 @ p1` prints the full
`1000000000000000019884624838656.0` in both). Analyst 2's 300,871-case randomized differential
test, including 40,000 exact dyadic ties, found zero mismatches.

---

## 8. UNTESTED-BY-THE-REFERENCE BEHAVIOR (decisions, flagged as such)

### 8.1 `total <= 0`: NO GUARD. Decided.
Do **not** add `if total <= 0 { return "0.0%".to_string() }` or any clamp/early return.
Reasons:
1. There is provably no guard in the C++ (no test/branch on `total` before `divss`), so a guard
   would invent behavior the reference does not have.
2. Unguarded IEEE `f32` division **matches C++ exactly** on the infinity cases: `1/0` → `inf`,
   `-1/0` → `-inf`, printing `inf%` / `-inf%` in both languages (re-verified in Rust this session).
   A guard would destroy a confirmed match.
3. No panic risk, because `ratio()` is float-only. This is the real reason the arithmetic must stay
   in floating point: an integer `progress * 100 / total` panics with "attempt to divide by zero"
   in both debug and release, which would violate "must not panic on any args".
4. `0/0` is the single known divergence: C++/glibc prints `-nan` (x86-64 `0.0f/0.0f` produces a
   negative quiet NaN; even the brief's "nan" is off), Rust prints `NaN` and ignores the sign bit.
   No Rust format flag produces lowercase `nan`, so parity would need an explicit
   `is_nan()`/`is_sign_negative()` shim. **Do not add the shim.** It is unreachable from these call
   sites and would be untestable ceremony.
The `ratio()` doc comment already records this decision in the source; keep that comment.

### 8.2 Other unexercised behavior, all left to plain IEEE + `{:.N$}`
No divergence exists for: negative ratios (`-33.3` in both), `-0.0` (`-0.0` in both), huge finite
values (full exact expansion in both, no scientific fallback), `f32::MAX * 100` (`inf` in both),
precision 0 (`33`, no decimal point, in both), large precision (matches at p=6).
`ProgressBuilder`'s `{0, 100}` defaults are unobservable and cannot affect output.

---

## 9. WARNING-FREEDOM CHECKLIST (all verified with `-D warnings`, both paths)

1. `self::` on every re-export in a `mod.rs`, `super::` on every sibling import — otherwise
   **edition-2015 E0432 breaks bare rustc only**. (Bare `rustc test5.rs` must not be given
   `--edition`; requirement (a) is literally `rustc test5.rs`.)
2. `_args`, not `args`.
3. No array `.into_iter()` anywhere (edition-2015 `array_into_iter` warning).
4. No `try` / `async` / `await` as identifiers (reserved in 2018+); no `panic!(non_str_expr)`.
5. Exactly one `#[allow(dead_code)]`, on `ProgressTracker::stages`, with the reason in a comment.
   No crate-level allow. No `#[allow]` on anything else.
6. Hand-written `Default` for both structs (deriving is impossible with `total = 100`) — trait impls
   are never reported as dead code, so they cost nothing.
7. No `[lib]` target; no `src/main.rs`; `[[bin]]` present.

---

